Body:
I'm also interested how to do this with the SDK/API 

I'm reverse-engineered the web ui a bit and replicate what it does 

(This is my code, not the webui)
```
		logger.info(`Opening repository project at: ${repositoryPath}`);
		const projectResponse = await fetch(`${this.baseUrl}/project/current`, {
			method: "GET",
			headers: {
				"x-opencode-directory": repositoryPath,
			},
		});
		logger.info(`Creating worktree from repository: ${repositoryPath}`);
		const response = await fetch(
			`${this.baseUrl}/experimental/worktree?directory=${encodeURIComponent(repositoryPath)}`,
			{
				method: "POST",
				headers: {
					"x-opencode-directory": repositoryPath,
				},
			},
		);
```

And this does work, but it doesn't really fully register the project with opencode it seems. The thing never appears in any sidebar.

The only way to access it is opening a session on it and going to the url of it. 
`/Lw/session/ses_$sessionId`

This works but the session doesn't really belong to anything.

Would love a way in the SDK to open projects

i also run into this issue

> 
> Temporary Fix: In your opencode.json, configure the compaction agent to use a smaller/cheaper model with a large context window:
> 
> {
>   "agent": {
>     "compaction": {
>       "model": "github-copilot-enterprise/claude-sonnet-4"
>     }
>   }
> }

I'm using with `github-copilot-enterprise/gpt-5.2-codex` for compaction because I had the same issue with sonnet.

> I have configured my `opencode.json` to remove Ctrl+C from both `app_exit` and `input_clear`:
> 
> {
>   "keybinds": {
>     "app_exit": "<leader>q",
>     "input_clear": "escape"
>   }
> }
> However, pressing Ctrl+C still terminates the application. The keybind configuration is completely ignored.

@JohanSpannare-if 

You now need to configure `tui.json` instead of `opencode.json` (I don't know from which version it has changed).

I got same issue with Notion official remote MCP server.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "mcp": {
    "notion": {
      "type": "remote",
      "url": "https://mcp.notion.com/mcp",
      "enabled": true
    }
  }
}
```

@ghost91- No, because you still can't use OpenCode offline, due to https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/10416#issuecomment-3805948859 and more.

If it can't work offline, then it's not private.

FWIW, I would bet money on the issues outlined being reintroduced, as the repo is largely vibe coded. As quickly as issues are resolved, they will reappear.
